                <VentilationSystem>

The complex world of Building Science can be summed up in one statement ............ "Build Tight &amp; Ventilate Right". New house construction has a consistent focus on this theme and almost all new housing in Yellowknife includes a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) system. When retrofitting existing houses, it is important to reduce excess air leakage (uncontrolled heat &amp; moisture loss) while being aware when a house may become under-ventilated and require some additional ventilation.



During my evaluation of your home, it appears your house is only moderated "over ventilated" and natural air leakage from your home will be reduced in conjunction with some of the recommended upgrades (ie: high efficiency furnace, new ESTAR bathroom fans, exterior wall insulation that includes an air barrier house wrap, new ESTAR windows &amp; patio door, air sealing around the ceiling lights, etc.). Should the house become slightly under-ventilated, you could either increase the amount of exhaust-only ventilation, or install an HRV that is connected to the furnace duct system - for distribution. The HRV would recover about 65% of the heat from the outgoing air and transfer it to the incoming air (ie: the current fresh air pipe connected to the top of your furnace would now be connected into the HRV to pre-heat that incoming air). 



Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



Depending on the number of energy efficiency upgrades you complete, I may recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. Let's continue to talk about this.



&lt;b&gt;Rebates:&lt;/b&gt;

There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 170 litre electric hot water tank. Should you consider a switch to a propane furnace, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider replacing this electric tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher (I modeled one that is 0.95).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,016&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace has an estimated efficiency of 83.4% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE). The dedicated combustion air pipe to the furnace room has been disconnected &amp; plugged, so the current furnace must draw its' combustion air from inside the basement.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This furnace would direct vent out the side wall of the house, eliminating the need for the chimney &amp; it would draw its' combustion air from outside rather than from the basement. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE and an ECM motor, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,067&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;24.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove Heat for the Basement&lt;/b&gt;



Your current furnace serves all three floors (including the basement) however is controlled by a thermostat on the main floor. The door to the basement appears to normally be in the closed position and there is an open stairwell from the main to 2nd floor.



You had indicated the main floor of the house appears to become quite warm while the 2nd floor will remain colder. I had outlined some strategies within my January 17 email for you to investigate some possible remedies for this (vent blockage, thermostat location &amp; closing some main floor vents).



For the basement, the amount of heat going down there is completely dependent on the ductwork design, so you may find it colder than you would like it to be. Many homeowners will install a wood pellet stove in their basements to provide thermostatically controlled heat just to that zone. Because your house is very air tight, this pellet stove will require a dedicated outside combustion air pipe sealed to the back of the pellet stove.



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved (if you upgrade your furnace) &amp; the cost of wood pellets purchased) if you were to use the wood pellet stove to heat the basement.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your wood pellet stove to heat the basement, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 285&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane furnace. An qualified wood pellet stove is eligible for &lt;b&gt;1/3 of the purchase price up to $ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home is currently quite air tight (ie: not alot of natural air leakage through the building envelope) and includes the following mechanical ventilation appliances:

        - a heat recover ventilator (HRV) - was not operating based on the control settings

        - three bathroom exhaust fans - should upgrade to ESTAR &amp; have timer switches installed

        - a clothes dryer that exhausts to the exterior - the vent was heavily frosted over

        - the kitchen range hood fan does not exhaust to the outside - maybe vent to outside if desired



My email on January 17 outlined many opportunities that deal specifically with air leakage (around the existing doors) and ventilation design, so I will not repeat all of this again within this report. We will spend a fair bit of time reviewing this further when we meet on January 25 !!



The bathroom fans should be upgrading to ENERGY STAR rated units, that seal better, use much less electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 3 bathroom fans, and the dryer, indicated a pressure drop of 6.4 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is a chimney vented model that is susceptible to back-drafting combustion gases into the house.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors, however the smoke detector on the main floor was disconnected. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



